Here's some sample Java code that demonstrates how to solve the equation (4 * 5) / (7 - 2) + (10 / 2) * 3:
```
import java.util.Stack;
public class EquationSolver {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String equation = "(4 * 5) / (7 - 2) + (10 / 2) * 3";
        double result = solveEquation(equation);
        System.out.println("Result: " + result);
    }
    public static double solveEquation(String equation) {
        Stack<Double> operands = new Stack<>();
        Stack<Character> operators = new Stack<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < equation.length(); i++) {
            char c = equation.charAt(i);
            if (Character.isDigit(c)) {
                double num = 0;
                while (i < equation.length() && Character.isDigit(equation.charAt(i))) {
                    num = num * 10 + (equation.charAt(i) - '0');
                    i++;
                }
                i--;
                operands.push(num);
            } else if (c == '(') {
                operators.push(c);
            } else if (c == ')') {
                while (operators.peek() != '(') {
                    double num2 = operands.pop();
                    double num1 = operands.pop();
                    char op = operators.pop();
                    double result = performOperation(num1, num2, op);
                    operands.push(result);
                }
                operators.pop();
            } else if (c == '+' || c == '-' || c == '*' || c == '/') {
                while (!operators.empty() && hasPrecedence(c, operators.peek())) {
                    double num2 = operands.pop();
                    double num1 = operands.pop();
                    char op = operators.pop();
                    double result = performOperation(num1, num2, op);
                    operands.push(result);
                }
                operators.push(c);
            }
        }
        while (!operators.empty()) {
            double num2 = operands.pop();
            double num1 = operands.pop();
            char op = operators.pop();
            double result = performOperation(num1, num2, op);
            operands.push(result);
        }
        return operands.pop();
    }
    public static boolean hasPrecedence(char op1, char op2) {
        if (op2 == '(' || op2 == ')') {
            return false;
        }
        if ((op1 == '*' || op1 == '/') && (op2 == '+' || op2 == '-')) {
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
    public static double performOperation(double num1, double num2, char op) {
        switch (op) {
            case '+':
                return num1 + num2;
            case '-':
                return num1 - num2;
            case '*':
                return num1 * num2;
            case '/':
                return num1 / num2;
        }
        return 0;
    }
}
```
This code uses a stack-based approach to evaluate the equation, with separate stacks for operands and operators. It handles parentheses, and follows the order of operations for evaluating the equation. The result is returned as a double value, which can handle decimal values.

