To add a sorting feature to your Scala application, you can use the `sortBy` method available on the `List` class. Here's an updated version of your code that allows users to sort the list by any of the three attributes (name, price, or category) and in ascending or descending order:
```
case class Product(name: String, price: Double, category: String)
val productList = List(
  Product("Widget A", 10.99, "Widgets"),
  Product("Widget B", 15.99, "Widgets"),
  Product("Gizmo X", 5.99, "Gizmos"),
  Product("Gizmo Y", 8.99, "Gizmos")
)
// Define a function to sort the list based on a given attribute and order
def sortProducts(products: List[Product], sortBy: String, ascending: Boolean): List[Product] = {
  val sortedList = sortBy match {
    case "name" => products.sortBy(_.name)
    case "price" => products.sortBy(_.price)
    case "category" => products.sortBy(_.category)
    case _ => products // If sortBy is not valid, return the original list
  }
  if (ascending) sortedList else sortedList.reverse
}
// Prompt the user for the sort criteria
println("Sort by (name, price, category):")
val sortBy = scala.io.StdIn.readLine()
println("Ascending? (true/false):")
val ascending = scala.io.StdIn.readBoolean()
// Sort the list and print the results
val sortedProducts = sortProducts(productList, sortBy, ascending)
for(product <- sortedProducts) {
  println(s"${product.name} - ${product.price} - ${product.category}")
}
```
