Here's an example of how the Naive Bayes Classifier can be implemented in JavaScript:
```javascript
// Importing the natural language processing library
const natural = require('natural');
// Creating a new classifier instance
const classifier = new natural.BayesClassifier();
// Training the classifier with spam and legitimate emails
classifier.addDocument('Buy now, limited time offer!', 'spam');
classifier.addDocument('Thank you for your purchase', 'legitimate');
classifier.addDocument('You have won a free vacation!', 'spam');
classifier.addDocument('Your order has been shipped', 'legitimate');
// Training the classifier
classifier.train();
// Classifying new emails
console.log(classifier.classify('Get rich quick scheme'));
console.log(classifier.classify('Your package has been delivered'));
// Generating a summary of customer reviews
const reviews = ['This product is great!', 'The customer service was terrible', 'I would recommend this product to anyone'];
const tokenizer = new natural.WordTokenizer();
// Pre-processing the reviews
const processedReviews = reviews.map(review => tokenizer.tokenize(review));
// Extracting features from the reviews
const features = new natural.TfIdf();
features.addDocument(processedReviews[0]);
features.addDocument(processedReviews[1]);
features.addDocument(processedReviews[2]);
// Generating a summary of the reviews
const summary = features.listTerms(0).concat(features.listTerms(1)).concat(features.listTerms(2));
console.log(summary);
```
In this example, we first import the natural language processing library and create a new instance of the Naive Bayes Classifier. We then train the classifier with a few spam and legitimate emails, and classify new emails as either spam or legitimate.
Next, we generate a summary of customer reviews by first pre-processing the reviews and then extracting features from them using the Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF) technique. Finally, we generate a summary of the reviews by listing the most important terms in each review.
To optimize the performance of the algorithm on large datasets and enable real-time processing, we can use techniques such as parallel processing, caching, and feature selection. Parallel processing involves dividing the dataset into smaller parts and processing them concurrently on multiple threads or machines. Caching involves storing the results of previous computations in memory to avoid redundant computations. Feature selection involves selecting only the most relevant features from the dataset to reduce the dimensionality of the problem.
To ensure the effectiveness of the algorithm in tackling new and emerging spam patterns, we can use techniques such as regular re-training, feature engineering, and ensemble learning. Regular re-training involves periodically re-training the algorithm on new data to keep it up-to-date. Feature engineering involves creating new features from the existing data to improve the algorithm's performance. Ensemble learning involves combining multiple algorithms to improve their performance and robustness.

