In this code, we have used the following tags:
- `<!DOCTYPE html>`: declares the document type and version
- `<html>`: the root element of the HTML document
- `<head>`: contains metadata such as the page title and CSS styles
- `<title>`: sets the page title that appears in the browser tab
- `<style>`: contains CSS styles for the page
- `<body>`: contains the visible content of the page
- `<header>`: contains the page header with the title
- `<h1>`: sets the main heading of the page
- `<img>`: displays the profile picture
- `<section>`: divides the page into sections
- `<h2>`: sets the section headings
- `<p>`: displays text content
- `<ul>` and `<li>`: create unordered lists
To make the page responsive to different screen sizes, we can use CSS media queries to adjust the layout and styling based on the device width. For example:
```
@media only screen and (max-width: 600px) {
